{"id":"3ba678e5-643a-4fcb-9095-a691569c5312","object":"chat.completion","created":1785006450,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = case['required_symbols']\\n    required_set = set(required)\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        child_list = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid not in removed_set for cid in child_list):\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_set:\\n            if symbol_counts[sym] <= 1:\\n                continue\\n        removed_set.add(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_set])\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree. The input case contains nodes with id, parent (null or string), symbol, depth, removable (bool), utility (float). Also required_symbols list. Output must have removed_node_ids (array of strings, order of removal) and retained_node_ids (array of strings, sorted? The contract says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted, removed_node_ids in order of removal.\n\nWe need algorithm: grammar-guided reduction. The description: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The oracle here is the required_symbols condition: we must keep at least one node with each required symbol. Also we must not remove root (non-removable?), non-removable nodes are already marked removable=false, so we cannot remove them. Also we must not remove a node that has retained children? That's tricky: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are being retained, we cannot remove that node? That means removal is only possible if all its children are also removed, or if it is a leaf? Actually probably \"a node with retained children\" means if we are considering removing a node, we must ensure that any children that are not also being removed are already removed? But removal order: we process nodes deeper first, so children are considered before parents. So when we consider a node, if it has children that are retained, then we cannot remove it because then those children would become orphaned? But the children are deeper, they might be removed or retained. The rule says never remove a node with retained children. So we can only remove a node if all its descendant nodes (in the subtree) that are to be retained? Actually if we remove a node, its children would become disconnected. So the only way to remove a node is if its entire subtree is also removed, otherwise we lose the retained children. Since we process bottom-up, when we consider a node, we have already decided which of its descendants are removed/retained. So we can only remove the node if none of its descendants are retained (i.e., the whole subtree is removed). Also we must ensure required symbols: if a required symbol appears only in the subtree of the node, and we remove the node and all its descendants, we lose that symbol. So we cannot remove a node that is the only provider of a required symbol (unless its descendants also have that symbol? But they are also removed, so no). So we need to check that after removal, each required symbol still appears in the retained set (i.e., some node with that symbol is not removed). But note: the required_symbols are symbols not node IDs. We need to ensure at least one node with each required symbol remains.\n\nAlso we have tie-breaking: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So if two nodes have same depth, we process in order of node ID? But removal order: we decide which nodes to remove, and then order the removed list by the order they were removed (should be deeper first, then shallower, and within same depth by node ID? Probably tie-break on node ID). Also we need to consider \"grammar_valid\": The reduction must be grammar-valid. Since we don't have a grammar, maybe the grammar validity is implicitly encoded by the parent-child relationships and symbol constraints? Or maybe we need to consider that we can only remove a node if its removal doesn't violate grammar? But the description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". Possibly we are not doing replacements, only deletions. Also \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar\". Typically, Perses reduces a parse tree by deleting nodes or replacing them with smaller alternatives while preserving validity according to a grammar and interestingness (like required symbols). Since we don't have the full grammar, the only constraints given are: nodes have parent relationships forming a tree, removable flag indicates whether the node can be considered for removal (some are forced retained). And required_symbols list indicates symbols that must appear at least once in the final tree.\n\nWe could adopt a greedy algorithm: start from leaves, go up (deeper first). For each node that is removable and not root, tentatively consider removing it. Check: after removal (i.e., if we mark it removed), does the tree still have at least one node for each required symbol? But removal of a node also implies removal of its entire subtree? Actually if we remove a node, we remove that node and all its descendants? In parse tree reduction, removing a node typically removes the entire subtree rooted at that node. But the description \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we consider removing just that node (and its subtree) and check if required symbols are still present in the rest of the tree. However, also \"Never remove a node with retained children\" implies that if we have decided to retain some descendant, we cannot remove the ancestor. So we need to decide removal in a bottom-up manner: process nodes from deepest to shallowest. For each node, we decide whether to retain or remove it. But if we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed (since we processed them before and they would have been removed already if we decided to remove them? Actually if we remove a node, its descendants are also removed regardless of their own decision, because they are no longer in the tree. So the decision for a node influences its children: if we decide to remove the node, its children are automatically removed. So we cannot independently decide for children if the parent is removed. Therefore the bottom-up processing should be: for each node (starting from deepest), we decide whether to remove it. If we remove it, then all its descendants are also considered removed (but we already processed them; we can mark them as removed). However, we need to consider that a descendant might have been earlier marked as retained, but then we later remove its ancestor, so that descendant is effectively removed. That violates consistency. So we need to process in a way that when we decide to remove a node, we also remove all descendants. That means we should process top-down or bottom-up with a constraint that once we decide to remove a node, we undo previous retentions in its subtree? Or we can process bottom-up and only remove a node if we have already decided to remove all its children (i.e., the entire subtree is removed). That aligns with \"Never remove a node with retained children\". So we should only remove a node if we have decided to remove all its children (i.e., the whole subtree is to be removed). Then the decision for children is made first. So algorithm:\n\n1. Build tree structure from nodes list (keyed by id). Find root (parent null).\n2. Determine ordering: sort nodes by depth descending (deeper first), and for same depth, by node ID (or stable, perhaps by id string). Process in that order.\n3. Maintain a set of removed_node_ids (initial empty). Also track which nodes are retained (initially all nodes not removed).\n4. For each node in the order:\n   - Skip if node is not removable (removable == false).\n   - Skip if node is root (id is root? root has parent null).\n   - Check: Are all children of this node already removed? Because if any child is retained, then removing this node would orphan that child (since child's parent reference would point to a removed node). But in abstract reduction, removing a node effectively removes its entire subtree, so if a child is retained, we cannot remove the parent because the child would be removed too. The condition \"Never remove a node with retained children\" means if any child is still in the retained set, we cannot remove this node. So we need to check: for each child of this node, is that child's id in removed_node_ids? If any child is not in removed_node_ids, then the child is currently retained, so we cannot remove the parent.\n   - Also check that after removing this node (and its descendants,